{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"nauseating-asphalt-quail","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_reads_under_sync_contention.py:152` bounds each `_rebar_cli` read\nat `timeout=10` while a background thread holds the tracker write lock. It is the same construct\nas `dire-negative-bunting` — a wall-clock bound doing duty as a behavioural oracle — with even\nthinner headroom, and it has NOT yet been observed failing.\n\n## Why it is a sibling, and why it was NOT fixed in the same change\n\nSame shape: hold the writer lock, run a read through the real CLI in a subprocess, and use the\nelapsed time to decide whether the read stalled. Its blocking signature is ~120s\n(`release.wait(timeout=120)`), so the bound discriminates in principle.\n\nBut the semantics DIFFER, which is why this is not a mechanical copy of that fix. Its docstring\nstates the product contract it is checking: 'each read waits <=2s for the lock then serves its\nlocal snapshot'. So `timeout=10` is testing a real 2s product deadline plus overhead — not merely\nliveness. Raising it the way `dire-negative-bunting` was raised would weaken a genuine product\nassertion. The right remedy needs a decision about how much of that 10s is the 2s guarantee and\nhow much is machine slack, and that is a different judgement from the one already made.\n\n## Additional exposure\n\nThe call sits inside a `for name, argv, shape, complete in SURFACES` loop, so several subprocess\ninvocations run under one held lock. Cumulative slowness on a constrained runner therefore has\nmore chances to cross the bound than the single-call case that already failed.\n\n## Evidence from the sibling investigation\n\nOn this machine (load 9.9) the analogous preview read completed in ~1.2s with the writer lock\nheld. The failing sibling case crossed 15s on a macOS CI runner — a >12x excursion. A 10s bound\nover a documented 2s target is 5x, i.e. thinner than the one that already broke.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide, and record, how the 10s splits between the 2s product deadline and machine slack.\n- [ ] The bound tolerates a CI-scale slowdown without weakening the <=2s guarantee — e.g. assert\n      the product deadline separately from the subprocess liveness bound, rather than conflating\n      them in one number.\n- [ ] The test still fails if a read genuinely stalls on the held lock (mutation-check it).\n- [ ] The per-surface loop's cumulative exposure is considered, not just the single-call case.\n\n## Found by\n\nThe Step 7 construct sweep while fixing `dire-negative-bunting`. Not observed failing; filed\nrather than fixed so the product-deadline question gets a deliberate answer.","id":"7f24-2ea5-e593-4ff1","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["tests","flake","ci","timing"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Sibling thin-headroom timing oracle: test_reads_complete_promptly_while_write_lock_is_held bounds reads at 10s"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787891075014008001,"uuid":"66871e97-1d47-43e2-8bcf-a53c9d3daa9b"}